------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE DYNAMICAL VERSION (a 1-D shell integration in a LambdaCDM background, not the static balance):
  r'' = -nu(g_N/a_0) g_N + Omega_L H_0^2 r,  started on the Hubble flow at a = 0.02, run to a = 1.
  R_0 = the radius of the shell whose radial velocity is zero today.  This is what an observer measures.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    M_b [Msun]   R_0 framework canon   R_0 framework alt   r_Lambda static canon  R_0/r_Lambda
          1e10                 1.048               1.098                   1.890         0.554
          1e11                 1.865               1.954                   3.362         0.555
          1e12                 3.322               3.480                   5.978         0.556
          1e13                 5.925               6.205                  10.631         0.557

  R_0 / r_Lambda = 0.555 +- 0.001 over three decades of mass -- so the STATIC
  closed form (K02) and the honest integration differ by a constant of order one, and (K02) can be used with
  that constant folded in.  That constant is the item's only non-predicted number and it is quoted here.
  [PASS] K02a the dynamical integration must reproduce the closed form's 1/4 mass slope -- if it does not, the static balance is not the right reading of the zero-velocity surface   (integrated d log R_0/d log M_b = 0.2508 against the predicted 0.2500)

