------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE EXTERNAL FIELD, which the framework does not let us ignore at Mpc separations
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  e_N = g_ext/a_0 = 0.000   ->  R_0(M_b = 2e11) = 2.218 Mpc
  e_N = g_ext/a_0 = 0.003   ->  R_0(M_b = 2e11) = 1.219 Mpc
  e_N = g_ext/a_0 = 0.010   ->  R_0(M_b = 2e11) = 1.015 Mpc
  e_N = g_ext/a_0 = 0.030   ->  R_0(M_b = 2e11) = 0.859 Mpc
  (the prescription used is nu(y + e_N), the simple scalar one; the repository's own item 8 found that this
   over-predicts relative to a careful QUMOND treatment, so these numbers are indicative, not a forecast.)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THE OBVIOUS BIG SAMPLE CANNOT BE USED (bug pattern 5, caught before it was committed)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Kourkchi & Tully 2017 tabulate a 'second turnaround radius' R_2t for 8826 groups -- the obvious sample.
  But log R_2t - (1/3) log M_K has an rms of 0.00136 dex over those 8826 groups.  R_2t is not a
  measurement; it is M_K^(1/3) with a fixed coefficient.  Correlating it with baryonic mass would have
  recovered the exponent 1/3 by construction and 'refuted' the framework with its own input.
  [PASS] K02e KT2017's turnaround radii are a DERIVED quantity and must not be used -- this check fires if the rms of log R_2t - (1/3) log M_K is small enough to prove they are not independent measurements   (rms 0.00136 dex -- deterministic to four decimal places)

