  stellar share of M_b in the Local Group: 0.864
  d log R_0(predicted) / d log Upsilon_K = +0.216   (= f_star/4, since R_0 ~ M_b^(1/4))
  inverted the other way, d log a_0 / d log Upsilon_K = -0.864 if R_0 is used as an a_0 meter
  [PASS] K02-UPS the test's Upsilon lever must be mild -- a quarter-power law on a mass that is only partly stellar is the single most Upsilon-insensitive place a_0 has been put in this hunt   (d log R_0/d log Upsilon = +0.216)
  [PASS] M02a quadrupling a_0 must move R_0 by exactly a factor 4^(1/4) = 1.414, because (K02) has a_0 to the quarter power   (measured 1.4117 against the predicted 1.4142)
  [PASS] M02b a hundredfold change in baryonic mass must move R_0 by exactly 100^(1/4) = 3.162 -- the estimator must not be a fixed point   (measured 3.173 against 3.162)

========================================================================================================================
THE RESTATEMENT TEST -- can (K02) be derived from v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra?
========================================================================================================================
  Attempt it.  v^4 = G M_b a_0 gives v_flat = (G M_b a_0)^(1/4).  Equation (K02) then reads R_0 = C v_flat/H_Lambda.
  So the MASS EXPONENT, 1/4, IS forced by the BTFR and is a restatement -- that half closes.
  What does NOT close: the BTFR contains no cosmology.  To get from v_flat to a radius you need the
  cosmological repulsion term Omega_L H_0^2 r and the collapse history, neither of which is anywhere in
  v^4 = G M_b a_0.  The coefficient C and the appearance of H_Lambda are new content.
  VERDICT: PARTIAL RESTATEMENT.  The exponent is the BTFR's; the coefficient is not.  And the coefficient is
  where the interesting claim lives, because a_0 = (c/2) sqrt(G rho_DE) makes H_Lambda and a_0 the same
  constant seen twice: R_0 = (G M_b)^(1/4) (c/(2 Z))^(1/4) H_Lambda^(1/4) / H_Lambda x C, i.e.
  R_0 propto (G M_b c / 2Z)^(1/4) H_Lambda^(-3/4).  Nobody has written that down.
  Numerically, with Z = sqrt(32 pi/3) = 5.7888: a_0/(c H_Lambda) = 0.1725 against 1/Z = 0.1727 -- the canonical footing IS cH_Lambda/Z by construction

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- k02
========================================================================================================================
  5 nearby groups have a usable zero-velocity radius in the Local Volume catalogue.
  The Local Group's comes out at 1.18 Mpc if the fit converged, against the published 0.96.
  The framework, fed only the measured stellar and cold-gas mass, predicts a median 2.03x (canonical) / 2.13x (alt) the measured radius.
  A ratio near 1 would be a second law with a_0 and Lambda on both sides of it.  A ratio far from 1 is a
  liability of the same family as item 13's Local Group timing over-prediction, and is reported as one.
  The item's own limits, stated: six groups, 1-2 decades of mass, a stellar+cold-gas mass that misses warm
  gas, an external-field prescription the repository has already found to be the optimistic one, and a
  zero-velocity fit whose intercept is sensitive to the radial window.  The mass SCALING -- 1/4 against
  1/3 -- is the discriminator and this sample cannot reach it.  What is decisive and cheap: the same
  measurement on the ~30 Local Volume groups with Hubble-flow coverage in the full UNGC.

RESULT: 9 checks, 1 FAIL -> ["K02c THE HEADLINE CHECK, AND IT CAN FAIL: the framework's zero-velocity radius, computed from the measured baryonic mass with no free parameter, must match the measured radius to better than a factor 1.5"]   rc=1
